= shui-tools

A small collection of terminal user interface building blocks for POSIX shell scripts.

The project currently provides four standalone programs:

* `shakey` — wait for a single key press
* `shselect` — interactive list selector and pager
* `shedit` — lightweight terminal text input widget
* `shbar` — terminal divider, status, or header bar

All tools are written in portable C99 and have no external dependencies beyond a POSIX-compatible operating system and terminal.

== Background

I had been missing simple terminal UI building blocks since around 2009, but never found the time to write them.

The goal of this project is to provide reusable components for shell scripts—selecting files, entering text, displaying simple menus, or controlling terminal applications—without relying on `tput`, `ncurses`, or large TUI frameworks.

The design follows a deliberately minimal approach:

* Small standalone executables
* Portable C99 source code with no external dependencies
* Simple compilation with any standard C compiler
* Practical building blocks for scripts and minimal systems

== Tested Platforms

The tools have been tested on:

* Debian GNU/Linux x86_64 (WSL2)
* NetBSD x86_64
* NetBSD arm64
* NetBSD i386

Other platforms should work as long as a reasonably POSIX-compatible terminal environment is available.

== Included Programs

=== `shakey`

Wait for a single key press without requiring Enter, optionally with a timeout. Useful for confirmation prompts, media player controls, keyboard shortcuts, or interruptible sleep loops.

Confirmation dialog:

[source,sh]
----
answer=$(shakey -s -p "Delete file? (y/n) ")

case "$answer" in
    y|Y) rm file.txt ;;
esac
----

Interruptible sleep loop:

[source,sh]
----
while true; do
    key=$(shakey -t 60 -s)
    [ "$key" = "q" ] && break

    check_for_updates
done
----

=== `shselect`

Interactive list selector and pager. Useful for menus, file selection, launchers, or choosing entries from piped output.

Simple menu:

[source,sh]
----
choice=$(
    printf "%s\n" "Play Music" "Settings" "Quit" |
        shselect -s -p "Main Menu"
)

case "$choice" in
    "Play Music") echo "Starting player" ;;
    "Settings")   echo "Opening settings" ;;
    "Quit")       exit 0 ;;
esac
----

Multiselect example:

[source,sh]
----
selected=$(find . -type f | shselect -m -s -p "Select files")
printf '%s\n' "$selected"
----

=== `shedit`

Simple multi-line text input widget for shell scripts. Useful for notes, commit messages, chat inputs, or reviewing generated text.

Interactive prompt:

[source,sh]
----
message=$(shedit -p "Enter message")
----

Preloading text or reviewing generated content:

[source,sh]
----
message=$(shedit -r10 < template.txt)

jq '.' data.json | shedit -r20 -p "Review JSON" > data-new.json
----

=== `shbar`

Visual divider, header, or bottom status bar that automatically fills the terminal width. Useful for separating command output, displaying status bars, or framing terminal headers.

Status bar with prompt text:

[source,sh]
----
status="[ load 3% : t/sec 49 : source.c ] "
shbar -p "${status}"
----

Reverse-video header bar with space padding:

[source,sh]
----
shbar -bp "Result as follows: " -c " "
cat result.txt | less -r +G
----

== Building and Installation

No `Makefile` is required. Compile each program individually using a C99 compiler:

[source,sh]
----
cc -s shakey.c   -o shakey   -std=c99 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra
cc -s shselect.c -o shselect -std=c99 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra
cc -s shedit.c   -o shedit   -std=c99 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra
cc -s shbar.c    -o shbar    -std=c99 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra
----

Install the binaries into your `PATH`:

[source,sh]
----
install -m755 shakey shselect shedit shbar /usr/local/bin/
----

Install the manual pages (and update the man database if required by your system):

[source,sh]
----
install -d /usr/local/share/man/man1
install -m644 man/shakey.1 man/shselect.1 man/shedit.1 man/shbar.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
mandb
----

== Documentation

Manual pages are located in the `man/` directory and can be read before or after installation:

[source,sh]
----
man ./man/shakey.1
man shakey
----

== Exit Codes

The tools follow standard UNIX conventions and return non-zero exit codes on errors. Some programs also use specific exit codes for script flow control—for example, `shselect` returns `10` when exited via `x`, making it easy to implement "back" navigation in menus. See the respective man pages for details.

